<%# THE FIRST-NAME STEP'S EMPTY-FIELD ERROR, in a browser.

    WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS AND A RENDER TEST DOES NOT COVER IT. The error copy is a
    JS string INSIDE the x-data. A view test can assert the sentence is present in
    that attribute; it cannot assert a user ever SEES it, and it stays green when
    the attribute is syntactically broken - which is exactly how this component
    fails. A stray quote closes the attribute early, Alpine mounts a silent no-op,
    and every element a string assertion looks for is still in the markup. Only a
    browser can tell those two pages apart.

    THE LAB RULE: this page sets up the partial's LOCALS and nothing else. The
    three modes ARE three sets of locals, so all three are the engine partial
    rendered by name.

    THE THIRD CARD CARRIES AN APOSTROPHE ON PURPOSE. empty_error is the first
    host-supplied PROSE to land inside the x-data, and prose is where the quote
    that kills the component comes from. If the escaping is ever dropped, that
    card mounts dead and shows no error at all.

    CARDS FOUR THROUGH EIGHT ARE THE REST OF THAT x-data'S LOCALS. empty_error was
    never the only one interpolated into the attribute - submit_path, skip_path and
    done_event land inside JS single-quoted literals exactly as it does, and
    modal_store lands in IDENTIFIER position, which is the same silent brick with a
    different repair. Each card feeds ONE of them a value that would close its
    literal, so a repair applied to one line cannot cover for a line it missed.

    THESE CARDS REACH THE STORE, and the three above do not. The empty path returns
    before finish() runs; a submit or a skip that SUCCEEDS calls close() on the
    host's store. So the specs install that store themselves - a store is not a
    local, it is what a host mounts around the card, and installing it in the spec
    is also what lets the spec assert WHICH store the card reached.

    RENDERED STANDALONE, not inside a modal host, and that is deliberate: the
    empty path never reaches the store. save() returns before it reads props or
    calls finish(), so a host would add a click to open each card and would grade
    the host - which lab/modal_host already does.
%>
<main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10 space-y-12" x-data>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-2">First-name step — the interpolated locals</h1>

  <section data-test="default-card">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">Default — the skippable card</h2>
    <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
               id: "lab-default-first-name" %>
  </section>

  <section data-test="required-card">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">required: true — the gated card</h2>
    <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
               id: "lab-required-first-name", required: true %>
  </section>

  <section data-test="custom-card">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">A host's own copy, apostrophe and all</h2>
    <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
               id: "lab-custom-first-name", required: true,
               empty_error: "We'll need your first name before you continue." %>
  </section>
  <%# submit_path, skip_path and done_event each sit inside a JS single-quoted
      literal in the x-data. Each card below carries the apostrophe and the double
      quote that end one, in exactly one of the three, so a spec that goes green
      names the local that survived. The paths point at the lab echo the spec
      fulfils, because a card that cannot complete its POST never reaches the
      finish() these specs read. %>
  <section data-test="hostile-submit-card">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">submit_path carrying a quote</h2>
    <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
               id: "lab-hostile-submit-first-name",
               submit_path: %q(/lab/echo?first=it's a "quote") %>
  </section>

  <section data-test="hostile-skip-card">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">skip_path carrying a quote</h2>
    <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
               id: "lab-hostile-skip-first-name",
               skip_path: %q(/lab/echo?skip=it's a "quote") %>
  </section>

  <%# The event NAME is the hostile value here; the skip path is the lab echo only
      so the skip can complete and dispatch it. A backslash rides along because it
      is the third character escape_javascript answers for and no URL parser is in
      the way of an event name. %>
  <section data-test="hostile-event-card">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">done_event carrying a quote</h2>
    <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
               id: "lab-hostile-event-first-name",
               skip_path: "/lab/echo?event-card=1",
               done_event: %q(it's a "done"\event) %>
  </section>

  <%# modal_store is the ODD ONE OUT and these two cards are why it gets its own
      pair. It is spliced in as a bare NAME, so a hostile value never reaches a
      browser at all - the partial refuses it at render, which the view test owns.
      What a browser can prove is the other half: that a host's own identifier is
      spliced VERBATIM and the card really talks to THAT store, at every site.
      Skippable reaches the props getter and finish()'s close; required reaches the
      third site, the Ruby-built @click on the x. %>
  <section data-test="custom-store-card">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">modal_store: a host's own store</h2>
    <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
               id: "lab-custom-store-first-name",
               skip_path: "/lab/echo?store-card=1",
               modal_store: "labModals" %>
  </section>

  <section data-test="custom-store-required-card">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3">modal_store: the required card's ×</h2>
    <%= render "studio/modals/onboarding/first_name",
               id: "lab-custom-store-required-first-name",
               required: true, modal_store: "labModals" %>
  </section>
</main>
